WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.770
Hello and welcome back to another
video with ItsDrDan and today we're

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:07.680
going to be learning about the tips
and tricks on how to convert a Haworth

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:12.929
projection to a Fischer projection
in introductory biochemistry.

00:00:13.459 --> 00:00:16.599
Now one of the ways that you learn
all about how to show different

00:00:16.599 --> 00:00:18.929
simple sugars or carbohydrates.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:23.189
is by drawing a Fischer projection,
just like the one shown here, where

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:26.519
you might draw the enantiomers, you
might label all the chiral carbons,

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:32.230
the epimers, the diastereomers, whether
it's an ketose, he'll show reactions.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:36.970
But as we realize that sugars do not
simply generally exist like this,

00:00:37.139 --> 00:00:41.760
we always show them as, uh, these
different type of ring structures over

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.980
here, known as a Haworth projection.

00:00:44.615 --> 00:00:48.465
Now, in most courses, they focus
on going from Fischer to Haworth,

00:00:48.485 --> 00:00:52.555
but very rarely do they talk about,
well, how do you go backwards?

00:00:52.575 --> 00:00:56.384
How do you disassemble it to be
able to label what sugar it is?

00:00:56.604 --> 00:00:58.465
What is the easiest way to do this?

00:00:58.474 --> 00:01:00.075
And that's what we're going
to show in this video.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:04.250
is the little tips and tricks step
by step how to break down one of

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:06.170
these in a very quick and easy way.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:09.190
We're going to go through two
different molecules to illustrate this.

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.299
So let's take a look at the different
steps and thank you so much for

00:01:12.299 --> 00:01:16.900
stopping by and let's look at and try
to learn and conquer how to do this.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:25.500
The first step that we're going to
be doing is we number our carbons

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.905
and the thing is when we are looking
at these rings We have to figure

00:01:28.905 --> 00:01:31.125
out what the anomeric carbon is.

00:01:31.554 --> 00:01:35.134
So whenever we are finding our
anomeric, we need to find what we

00:01:35.135 --> 00:01:38.774
call a hemiacetal or a hemiketal.

00:01:38.774 --> 00:01:40.304
So we're looking for a signature.

00:01:40.734 --> 00:01:42.955
And whenever we're looking for
this, I always like to say we're

00:01:42.965 --> 00:01:46.865
looking for the little set of googly
eyes that you see in the molecule.

00:01:47.325 --> 00:01:52.205
So for example, if I kind of have one
of, uh, these little hemiacetals here,

00:01:52.205 --> 00:01:55.665
you can kind of just imagine that has
two little eyeballs on it with some

00:01:55.665 --> 00:01:57.735
little kind of little eyebrows above it.

00:01:58.125 --> 00:02:00.525
And these are your eyeballs that are here.

00:02:00.525 --> 00:02:03.155
You're looking for the two oxygens.

00:02:03.545 --> 00:02:07.475
that are on these molecules and
they're both connected to the anomeric

00:02:07.505 --> 00:02:11.695
carbon, which is right here as I'm
kind of showing here in purple.

00:02:12.075 --> 00:02:13.305
Um, it's that little dot.

00:02:13.365 --> 00:02:14.844
Those are your anomerics.

00:02:15.214 --> 00:02:19.144
So if we go to the structure over here,
you want to try to give the anomeric

00:02:19.174 --> 00:02:20.975
carbon the lowest number possible.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.780
And what we can see here as well,
you can notice that we do have an

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:29.430
anomeric carbon where those two O's
are connecting to this single point.

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:31.950
So this is what we're going
to give the number one.

00:02:31.999 --> 00:02:33.819
So we number around the ring.

00:02:33.829 --> 00:02:38.549
So it goes one, two, three, four, five,
and we're going to number it as if it's a

00:02:38.549 --> 00:02:41.655
chain, and continue the numbering outside.

00:02:41.655 --> 00:02:42.394
So number six.

00:02:42.394 --> 00:02:44.865
So one, two, three, four, five, and six.

00:02:45.484 --> 00:02:48.725
Now other things that are really
important to to try to remember as

00:02:48.725 --> 00:02:52.564
well is whether or not it's alpha
or beta because sometimes in your

00:02:52.564 --> 00:02:54.604
course you might have to label that.

00:02:55.204 --> 00:02:57.464
Now it's pretty
straightforward how to do it.

00:02:57.934 --> 00:03:01.424
So if you kind of look at let's
say this OH here that I'm now

00:03:01.435 --> 00:03:05.095
highlighting in teal and the CH5OH.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:07.100
Notice how they're both together.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:08.530
Together meaning same.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:09.420
This is cis.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:14.009
So in this case, if they're both
pointing up together or both pointing

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:16.750
down together, that's referring to cis.

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:21.359
So what we have here is a beta ring
structure because they are together.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.590
If the OH was, let's say if it was
pointing on the other end, and if I

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:30.475
were to switch it, let's say I put
it here, and it was pointing down.

00:03:30.565 --> 00:03:32.745
Now they're both opposite of each other.

00:03:33.165 --> 00:03:36.315
This is referred to as alpha or trans.

00:03:37.005 --> 00:03:39.644
In order to kind of remember
those, there's a little trick that

00:03:39.684 --> 00:03:41.435
I learned that I kind of like.

00:03:41.925 --> 00:03:45.684
If you kind of look at the alpha symbol
and I put like little dots going down

00:03:45.684 --> 00:03:50.014
the side, notice how essentially the
little tails of the alpha are both

00:03:50.035 --> 00:03:52.395
pointing in opposite directions.

00:03:52.445 --> 00:03:54.135
That's how you can kind of remember trans.

00:03:54.704 --> 00:03:58.625
Whereas with cis, the little,
notice how the two humps of

00:03:58.625 --> 00:04:01.125
the B are together, right?

00:04:01.155 --> 00:04:03.205
They both are together
like a little heart.

00:04:03.595 --> 00:04:04.665
They go together, right?

00:04:04.675 --> 00:04:08.395
Step two, we're going to draw
the Fischer projection backbone.

00:04:08.874 --> 00:04:12.015
Now before we can do that, we have
to be able to label whether this

00:04:12.015 --> 00:04:16.535
is an aldose, meaning an aldehyde,
or a ketose, meaning a ketone.

00:04:17.034 --> 00:04:19.654
Now the way that we do this,
we look at the anomeric carbon

00:04:19.675 --> 00:04:21.294
and we count where it was.

00:04:21.719 --> 00:04:25.349
So if it's at number one, that
means that it's an aldehyde.

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:27.950
If it's at number two, it's a ketose.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:32.209
Now when we numbered the structure
from earlier, we saw that at number

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:34.620
one, this is our anomeric carbon.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.600
meaning that we're
going to have an aldose.

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:40.610
So what you're going to first
do is you're going to write

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:42.150
that you have an aldose here.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:46.730
So you have C double bond O H and we're
going to write that on our structure.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:52.000
Now the next thing is we're going
to draw our lines for our backbone.

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:56.350
So what I do is I just draw a little
spine here and you're going to end

00:04:56.350 --> 00:05:02.610
it with the CH2OH which is on carbon
number six from our original structure.

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:03.020
Okay.

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:07.250
Now All the other spots
are your chiro caron.

00:05:07.284 --> 00:05:10.675
So 2, 3, 4, and five are all chiro carons.

00:05:11.155 --> 00:05:15.055
So you need to draw a little flat
line for each of these spots.

00:05:15.055 --> 00:05:19.305
So we have 2, 3, 4, and then five.

00:05:19.335 --> 00:05:21.045
Okay, so we have each of those spots.

00:05:21.045 --> 00:05:23.115
One, two, let's number them as well.

00:05:23.115 --> 00:05:28.405
So we have 1, 2, 3, 4, 5,
and the last one is six.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:34.480
Now the last thing is, before we move
on to our next step, is, depending on

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:41.290
where that CH2OH is, at number 6, if it's
pointing up or down, tells you where your

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:44.869
last chiral OH is, on carbon number 5.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:50.250
So, if it was pointing up, that
means that it's a D enantiomer.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:54.860
If it's pointing down, it's an L
in the antimer, which is really

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:57.780
important because that tells you,
hey, which direction it's going to be.

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:03.720
On our original structure, you can see
that the CH2OH here was pointing up.

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:07.100
So what that's going to let you know
is, hey, we're going to put this

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:10.090
going to the right on our structure.

00:06:10.090 --> 00:06:13.039
So we have one of our pieces here.

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:17.180
Let's go into the next step to see a quick
and easy way to fill in the rest of this.

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:21.490
Step number three is what I consider a
pro tip being I've done so many of these

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:23.160
in my own personal teaching career.

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:27.780
The easiest way to do this is if
you draw a line down the bold part

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:31.080
of the Haworth projection, which
shows the wedge characteristic.

00:06:31.655 --> 00:06:33.255
So, which is on the bottom here.

00:06:33.655 --> 00:06:37.775
So what I do is whenever I'm visually
looking at these, I draw a little

00:06:37.785 --> 00:06:39.924
imaginary line down the middle.

00:06:39.935 --> 00:06:43.914
So if I do that here, so where this
little wedge is, I'm drawing these

00:06:43.915 --> 00:06:48.275
little dots to kind of illustrate how
I'm going to be looking at the structure.

00:06:48.855 --> 00:06:52.975
Now depending on where everything
is, you can actually quickly see how

00:06:52.975 --> 00:06:55.215
to fill in your is your projection.

00:06:55.685 --> 00:06:57.385
that is on the left here.

00:06:57.825 --> 00:07:01.785
So what we're going to do is, is
once you write that line, we are

00:07:01.785 --> 00:07:04.295
just going to turn it slightly.

00:07:04.335 --> 00:07:07.794
So we're going to turn it just like this.

00:07:08.555 --> 00:07:10.645
And what it's going to do, so I turned it.

00:07:10.990 --> 00:07:11.520
back.

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:14.990
So I turned it 90 degrees to the left.

00:07:15.599 --> 00:07:20.320
And what this is going to, this actually
shows you is where to put all your OHs.

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:25.280
So if we look at carbon number two, notice
how the OH is pointing to the right.

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:26.320
So that's what I'm going to do.

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:26.920
I'm going to write OH.

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:29.459
I'm going to put that
pointing to the right.

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:29.760
Okay.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:30.219
So OH.

00:07:30.905 --> 00:07:31.305
H.

00:07:31.685 --> 00:07:36.485
Now on carbon number three, notice how
the OH is pointing to the left hand side.

00:07:36.715 --> 00:07:40.065
So I'm going to put an
OH pointing to the left.

00:07:40.935 --> 00:07:45.275
Now on carbon number four, it's on the
right hand side of my dotted line, so

00:07:45.275 --> 00:07:47.335
we're going to fill that in as well.

00:07:47.854 --> 00:07:49.784
Now we have our complete diagram.

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:50.719
structure.

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:53.930
Now if you're not able to turn that on
your own very easily, you can always

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:58.250
turn your paper or your notebook
just to kind of show how to do that.

00:07:58.799 --> 00:08:04.059
Another easy way to do it is that if it's
on the upper side of the dotted line,

00:08:04.539 --> 00:08:11.949
Is that upper means equals left, and if
it's down, that means that equals right.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:15.069
Okay, when it comes to filling in
all these other chiral carbons.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:18.810
Do not get it confused with carbon
number five, that one's different.

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:24.419
So let's try to identify which
molecule this is, which is the last

00:08:24.419 --> 00:08:28.140
step is we're going to name and
identify our Fischer projection.

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:32.630
Our next step is that we're going to
name and identify what our structure is.

00:08:33.064 --> 00:08:35.885
When you're trying to identify
these, it can be really complicated,

00:08:35.885 --> 00:08:39.034
especially when you have a lot of
different molecules in front of you.

00:08:39.505 --> 00:08:42.804
The first thing I would recommend
doing is classifying your structure,

00:08:43.164 --> 00:08:47.725
meaning that we have an Aldo Hexoses,
so it's an aldehyde with six carbons.

00:08:48.305 --> 00:08:51.305
And that will help you limit down
all the types of diastereomers and

00:08:51.305 --> 00:08:53.495
nanotumors you're going to be looking at.

00:08:54.225 --> 00:08:59.855
We have, we also know that we have
a beta, or in our case was a beta D

00:08:59.864 --> 00:09:01.715
version of this structure as well.

00:09:01.935 --> 00:09:06.234
You know, a cis in the beginning, and
our last OH group is going to the right.

00:09:06.824 --> 00:09:10.425
So the quick way to look at this
structure is how to identify it, is

00:09:10.445 --> 00:09:12.345
I look at one side of the molecule.

00:09:12.775 --> 00:09:18.175
So in this case, what I'm looking
at is I see that I have OH, HOH.

00:09:18.805 --> 00:09:18.955
Oh.

00:09:19.585 --> 00:09:21.235
So I'm gonna match one side.

00:09:21.625 --> 00:09:25.075
So when we're looking, we're
gonna try to find OH, HOH.

00:09:25.485 --> 00:09:28.215
So if I look at, let's say like
looking at the first one, right?

00:09:28.215 --> 00:09:29.475
Allo doesn't match.

00:09:29.765 --> 00:09:31.055
Outros doesn't match.

00:09:31.345 --> 00:09:32.845
Wait, that's H-O-H-O-H.

00:09:33.315 --> 00:09:37.725
Now if I look at glucose,
that one is OH, HO, HOH.

00:09:38.225 --> 00:09:42.245
So that is a quick way to kind of identify
like, hey, do you have the right one?

00:09:42.245 --> 00:09:44.975
So you could also circle,
let's say the ohs as well,

00:09:45.214 --> 00:09:46.954
and see like, okay, which one.

00:09:47.329 --> 00:09:49.760
matches the same pattern.

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:53.830
So you're trying to figure out like,
okay, where is everything situated?

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:59.540
So what this tells us is the one that
we're working on is beta D glucose,

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:01.670
which is the name of our molecule.

00:10:02.129 --> 00:10:05.339
Let's take a look at another
one to practice looking at

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:07.374
all these configurations
and putting it all together.

00:10:07.475 --> 00:10:09.895
Okay, let's put it all together
going through our steps.

00:10:09.905 --> 00:10:13.365
So here we have, I want you to draw
the Fischer projection and name it.

00:10:13.445 --> 00:10:14.175
How would you do it?

00:10:14.725 --> 00:10:18.075
So the first thing we said to do is we're
going to number our carbons and try to

00:10:18.075 --> 00:10:20.175
give the anomeric the lowest one possible.

00:10:20.725 --> 00:10:23.974
So once again, what we're going to do is
we're looking for our little eyeballs.

00:10:24.494 --> 00:10:27.635
And what you can see here is
looking at the structure is right

00:10:27.645 --> 00:10:30.615
here is that you have the two OHS.

00:10:31.075 --> 00:10:33.975
Both kind of meeting here
at the exact same spot.

00:10:34.415 --> 00:10:38.965
So what this tells you is that
here is your anomeric carbon, we'll

00:10:38.965 --> 00:10:40.505
use a little star to tell us that.

00:10:41.115 --> 00:10:43.535
Now one thing you'll notice
is that you don't want to just

00:10:43.555 --> 00:10:45.105
automatically assume it's number one.

00:10:45.394 --> 00:10:49.425
You gotta look if there's any
carbons that are currently next to

00:10:49.425 --> 00:10:50.775
it, and we see that we have one.

00:10:51.235 --> 00:10:54.645
So meaning that this one's a
ketose, it's starting at this point.

00:10:55.435 --> 00:10:57.705
So what we'll do is we
number that number one.

00:10:58.094 --> 00:10:59.255
We go in number two.

00:10:59.734 --> 00:11:05.385
Number three number four and then we have
number five and we follow the carbons

00:11:05.385 --> 00:11:09.995
around as if it's in a chain Right,
the ring is also can exist as a chain.

00:11:10.784 --> 00:11:15.395
So you need to show both So we have
six carbons and it we notice that

00:11:15.395 --> 00:11:21.510
starting at CH2OH So what we're going
to do is write CH2OH OH at the top.

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:25.400
Now being it's a carbon number
two, that's letting you know, hey,

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:27.070
we have a ketone in this case.

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:31.410
So we have C double bond O, and what
we're going to do is just to kind

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:35.800
of help illustrate that we'll keep
our numbering with the same colors.

00:11:35.809 --> 00:11:37.360
So we got one, two.

00:11:38.419 --> 00:11:43.960
Now at the very end of the chain, we
know it's CH2OH is our last piece.

00:11:45.199 --> 00:11:47.620
Okay, so now we have all of our pieces.

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:49.300
We'll put in our chiral carbons.

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:51.589
We should have a total of six total.

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:55.389
So we have one, two,
three, four, five, six.

00:11:55.980 --> 00:11:59.250
All right, now our last step was
putting that little dotted line in

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:01.739
place to determine where the OHs go.

00:12:02.449 --> 00:12:07.620
So I'm going to put a little dots here
across the bottom to kind of help us

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:12.800
see the trend because we need to figure
out where does carbon three and four go.

00:12:13.175 --> 00:12:14.245
on this molecule.

00:12:14.315 --> 00:12:18.305
And the same thing with carbon number
5 as well, which was our last CH2OH.

00:12:18.855 --> 00:12:23.324
So if we go in order, carbon
number 3 is going down, which means

00:12:23.324 --> 00:12:25.214
that it's on the right hand side.

00:12:26.505 --> 00:12:30.714
And if it helps to, we can turn the
molecule as well to help show that.

00:12:30.755 --> 00:12:35.425
What we can see is that, okay, the first
OH is pointing to the right hand side.

00:12:35.994 --> 00:12:39.795
On carbon number 4, it's pointing
towards the opposite, which is the left.

00:12:40.185 --> 00:12:41.525
So let's do that here.

00:12:41.525 --> 00:12:43.404
So we have, already have 3 filled in.

00:12:43.405 --> 00:12:43.484
Okay.

00:12:43.954 --> 00:12:46.565
Number four, we're going to put O, then H.

00:12:47.065 --> 00:12:51.155
Now, carbon 5 depends on where
is the last CH2OH pointing.

00:12:51.165 --> 00:12:52.575
Is it pointing up or down?

00:12:53.004 --> 00:12:54.985
In this case, it's pointing down.

00:12:55.055 --> 00:12:58.715
So that means that it's going
to be on the left hand side,

00:12:59.015 --> 00:13:00.105
is where that's going to go.

00:13:00.624 --> 00:13:02.375
So we are going to put that in.

00:13:02.624 --> 00:13:05.695
So it's telling you, hey,
you have an L configuration.

00:13:06.234 --> 00:13:07.574
in your molecule.

00:13:08.125 --> 00:13:09.395
So let's kind of write that.

00:13:09.405 --> 00:13:11.335
And we also have alpha as well.

00:13:11.335 --> 00:13:14.545
So we have alpha, uh, alpha L.

00:13:14.624 --> 00:13:18.724
And the way that I could tell if it's
alpha is we see we have one OH pointing

00:13:18.755 --> 00:13:21.584
up, the other one pointing down.

00:13:21.584 --> 00:13:23.074
So they're both opposite sides.

00:13:23.764 --> 00:13:27.244
So let's try to name and
identify what we have.

00:13:27.935 --> 00:13:33.595
So if I were to do this, the thing
is that's more common is more, most

00:13:33.595 --> 00:13:39.295
monosaccharides tend to be given to
you as the D configuration, right?

00:13:39.855 --> 00:13:42.655
In most reference sheets, they
are the more common because you

00:13:42.655 --> 00:13:47.184
biologically tend to find L, or
sorry, D configuration in the body.

00:13:48.035 --> 00:13:52.905
So, being that we have an L carbon
here, right, because the OH is on

00:13:52.975 --> 00:13:58.345
the left, what we have to do is we
have to draw its mirror image first.

00:13:58.835 --> 00:14:03.135
So if we were to do that, so let's
kind of make our little mirror here,

00:14:03.814 --> 00:14:08.855
and what we're going to do is we're
going to copy this down, and flip all

00:14:08.875 --> 00:14:12.315
of the chiral carbons in our molecule.

00:14:12.945 --> 00:14:17.095
So we'll redraw this, just like,
redraw the backbone just like so.

00:14:17.185 --> 00:14:21.055
Now we're going to fill in all of
our O H's, H's to mirror each other.

00:14:21.555 --> 00:14:26.494
So what we have here is we have
O H, then O H, O H, and I'll fill

00:14:26.505 --> 00:14:31.145
in all the hydrogens, H, H, H.

00:14:31.265 --> 00:14:34.765
That way we have both the
L and the D configurations.

00:14:34.775 --> 00:14:36.930
So we've got L, And then D.

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:43.420
All right, so on our D configuration,
what we need is we need OH left, we need

00:14:43.420 --> 00:14:45.920
left, right, right for our configuration.

00:14:46.469 --> 00:14:50.400
So if we were to look here, left,
right, right, what we can see really

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:55.169
quickly is that fructose matches
left, right, right here at the bottom.

00:14:55.560 --> 00:15:00.420
So that's letting you know that, oh,
your structure that you have is fructose.

00:15:00.970 --> 00:15:05.689
So to name our original molecule,
this is going to be alpha L.

00:15:06.314 --> 00:15:08.985
Fructose is going to be the name of it.

00:15:09.485 --> 00:15:13.595
So this is my, my tips and tricks on
how to do these, how to go backwards.

00:15:14.075 --> 00:15:17.045
So hopefully this helped you a
little bit to see, like, just draw

00:15:17.045 --> 00:15:18.275
a little line down the middle.

00:15:18.454 --> 00:15:20.464
It makes it a lot faster to do it, right.

00:15:20.464 --> 00:15:21.995
You can do it in just a few minutes.

